Q10 coordinatewise meet on (step, -L, G) against the order-theoretic meet
    these are DIFFERENT claims: one asks whether a formula's output is a shape,
    the other asks whether a greatest lower bound exists at all.
    pairs compared 351   (singleton sources skipped: 27)
      componentwise triple IS a shape in the family:     155
      componentwise triple is NOT a shape in the family: 196
      UF<0,1> & UF<1,0>: componentwise (step,-L,G)=(Fraction(1, 1), Fraction(0, 1), Fraction(1, 2)) names no shape; the actual meet is UF<0,0>
      UF<0,1> & UF<2,0>: componentwise (step,-L,G)=(Fraction(1, 1), Fraction(0, 1), Fraction(1, 2)) names no shape; the actual meet is UF<0,0>
      UF<0,1> & UF<3,0>: componentwise (step,-L,G)=(Fraction(1, 1), Fraction(0, 1), Fraction(1, 2)) names no shape; the actual meet is UF<0,0>
      UF<0,1> & UF<4,0>: componentwise (step,-L,G)=(Fraction(1, 1), Fraction(0, 1), Fraction(1, 2)) names no shape; the actual meet is UF<0,0>
    the order-theoretic meet existed for every pair (Q8, origin admitted), so a
    closure condition stated for the FORMULA is not a condition on the meet.

