Q12 cross-radix joins: structural or artifact?
    cross-radix pairs 149   with no common upper bound in the space 96
      of those, the required step is neither a power of 1/2 nor of 1/3: 60
      that count is structural: no enlargement of either radix's box supplies
      a step with both 2 and 3 in its denominator.

Q13 the meet stops being EXACT once two sign domains share one order
    unsigned alone: meets exact 210, meets strictly undershooting the intersection 0
    unsigned + symmetric: meets exact 701, meets strictly undershooting the intersection 40
      U<0,2> & S<0,2>
        set intersection      [Fraction(0, 1), Fraction(1, 4)]
        greatest lower bound  [Fraction(0, 1)]   <- a proper subset of it
      U<0,2> & S<0,3>
        set intersection      [Fraction(0, 1), Fraction(1, 4)]
        greatest lower bound  [Fraction(0, 1)]   <- a proper subset of it
      U<0,2> & S<0,4>
        set intersection      [Fraction(0, 1), Fraction(1, 4)]
        greatest lower bound  [Fraction(0, 1)]   <- a proper subset of it

Q14 what the meet needs once a bias is admitted
    pairs 1326
      no greatest lower bound because the intersection is EMPTY:    670
      no greatest lower bound though the intersection is NONEMPTY:  130
      U<0,1>+1/2 & U<1,0>+0: intersection [Fraction(1, 1)] names no shape in the space
      U<0,1>+1/2 & U<2,0>+0: intersection [Fraction(1, 1)] names no shape in the space
      U<0,1>+1/2 & U<3,0>+0: intersection [Fraction(1, 1)] names no shape in the space
    so a biased space needs an empty numeral for the first count and a singleton
    at every reachable phase for the second. They are two separate admissions.

